<section class="sec sec-warm" aria-labelledby="preparing-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">God was preparing my heart</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title" id="preparing-h">Years later, God brought me <em>full circle</em>.</h2>
    </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
      <p>For the past four years, I have had the privilege of volunteering with a discipleship program serving men and women recovering from drug and alcohol addiction. Through this experience, I have witnessed the life-changing power of the Holy Spirit as people surrender their lives to God and begin to discover hope, purpose, and a new way of living.</p>

      <p>I have watched God restore relationships, redeem broken places, and remind people that their past does not have to determine their future. Over and over again, I have witnessed that God is a God of restoration.</p>

      <p>But one conversation with a resident changed the way I saw the needs of women transitioning out of recovery. She shared her uncertainty about what would happen when she completed the program. Where would she go next? How would she get to work, attend appointments, or manage everyday responsibilities without reliable transportation?</p>

      <p>As I listened, her questions stayed with me. I thought about my own journey years earlier. I remembered the transitional home, the resources, the volunteers, and the people God had placed in my life to help me rebuild. I knew firsthand how much those resources mattered.</p>

      <p>And I began to wonder. What happens when a woman is ready to move forward but is not quite ready to navigate independent living on her own? What if there could be a place where she did not have to figure it all out alone?</p>

      <p><strong>The difficult chapter I once lived through was becoming part of my calling. And in June 2024, Oasis Women Center was born.</strong></p>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec sec-paper" aria-labelledby="bridge-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">A bridge to a new beginning</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title" id="bridge-h">I want Oasis to be <em>a place of hope</em>.</h2>
    </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
      <p>Oasis Women Center exists to help women transitioning from substance-use recovery move toward sustainable independent living in a safe, Christ-centered environment. We want Oasis to be the bridge between completing a recovery program and building a sustainable, independent life.</p>

      <p>Through practical resources, life skills, financial literacy, employment and educational support, transportation connections, accountability, and community, we want to help women take their next steps with confidence.</p>

      <p>But more than providing a place to live, I want Oasis to be a place of hope. A place where women can rebuild their confidence, discover who they are in Christ, and begin to believe that their future can look different from their past.</p>

      <p>Because I know what it feels like to need someone to help you find your next step. Someone once helped me. Now, I want to help another woman find hers.</p>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec sec-clay" aria-labelledby="part-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="railed">
      <div class="rail reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">You are part of the story</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title" id="part-h">If you are reading this, <em>you may have a part to play</em>.</h2>
    </div>
      <div class="editorial reveal d1">
      <p>You may have time to volunteer, skills to share, a heart for mentoring, or the ability to help with transportation and essential resources. Perhaps you can donate furniture, supplies, or financial resources to help make this vision a reality.</p>

      <p>Whatever you have been given, you have an opportunity to use it to make a difference in the life of a woman rebuilding hers. They will need someone willing to say, "Keep going. God is not finished with you yet."</p>

      <p>Ultimately, this is not my story. It is His. He opened doors for me when I did not know where to turn. He provided when I did not know how I would move forward. And now, I believe He is inviting me to help open doors for women who are still finding their way.</p>

      <p><strong>Because someone once helped me, I know the difference that support can make. Now, together, we can help someone else.</strong></p>

      <p class="signoff">With gratitude and faith,<br><span>Keisha Ortiz</span><br>Founder, Oasis Women Center</p>
